  "subtitle": "Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine, even a table or a street. Most nouns ending in -o are masculine (el libro) and most ending in -a are feminine (la casa). Learn each noun with its article.",
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-04)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "el / la", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "noun", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">el</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">libro</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">la</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">casa</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">el</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">médico</span> · <span class=\"vp-prep\">la</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">médica</span>",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Nouns ending in <strong>-o</strong> are usually masculine: <em>el libro, el piso, el trabajo</em>. Nouns ending in <strong>-a</strong> are usually feminine: <em>la casa, la mesa, la cita</em>.",
        "tag": "-o and -a"
      },
      {
        "html": "These endings are feminine: <strong>-ción, -sión, -dad, -tad, -tud</strong>: <em>la estación, la ciudad, la salud</em>. Many nouns ending in <strong>-ma</strong> come from Greek and are masculine: <em>el problema, el idioma, el sistema</em>.",
        "tag": "Useful endings"
      },
      {
        "html": "Learn these exceptions early: <em><strong>el</strong> día, <strong>el</strong> mapa, <strong>el</strong> sofá</em> and <em><strong>la</strong> mano, <strong>la</strong> foto, <strong>la</strong> moto, <strong>la</strong> radio</em>.",
        "tag": "Exceptions"
      },
      {
        "html": "Nouns ending in <strong>-e</strong> or a consonant give no clear clue: <em>el coche, la calle, el hospital, la luz</em>. Learn them with the article.",
        "tag": "No clue"
      },
      {
        "html": "For people, the word often changes: <em>el profesor → la profesora, el médico → la médica</em>. Some words stay the same and only the article changes: <em>el estudiante, la estudiante</em>.",
        "tag": "People"
      },
      {
        "html": "Only <strong>names</strong> of people and places take a capital letter: <em>Lucía, Madrid, España</em>. Days, months and languages take a small letter: <em>lunes, mayo, español</em>.",
        "tag": "Small letters"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>la problema</em> → ✓ <em>el problema</em>. ✗ <em>el mano</em> → ✓ <em>la mano</em>. ✗ <em>Lunes, Español</em> in the middle of a sentence → ✓ <em>lunes, español</em>. Next: <a href=\"/articles\">Articles</a> and <a href=\"/plurals\">Plurals</a>.",
